<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/check/5> <https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/example> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/ontology#Rewrite> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/example> <https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/ontology#after> "The disciples end by asking who Jesus is, since even wind and sea obey him; the passage first shows his identity and calls me to trust him in the storm, whether or not it stops." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/example> <https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/ontology#before> "This story promises that Jesus will make the storms in my life go away if I have enough faith." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/example> <https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/ontology#situation> "A small group opens Mark 4:35-41, the stilling of the storm, during a hard season for one of its members." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/example> <https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/ontology#why> "Observing the closing question (Mark 4:41) and its echo of God calming the sea in Psalm 107:29 moves the group from a quick application to the passage's own focus on who Jesus is." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/howto/1> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/howto/1> <https://schema.org/position> "1"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/howto/1> <https://schema.org/text> "Pray for understanding, then choose a unit to study (a paragraph, a chapter or a whole short book) and read it through several times without notes or commentary." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/howto/2> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/howto/2> <https://schema.org/position> "2"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/howto/2> <https://schema.org/text> "Observe before you explain - write down who is involved, what happens or is argued, where and when, and note repeated words, contrasts, questions, turning points and the kind of writing it is." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/howto/3> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/howto/3> <https://schema.org/position> "3"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/howto/3> <https://schema.org/text> "Turn your observations into questions (what does this mean, why is it here, why is it said this way?) and answer them first from the passage and its surrounding book." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/howto/4> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/howto/4> <https://schema.org/position> "4"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/howto/4> <https://schema.org/text> "Only after your own study, check your conclusions against a good study Bible, Bible dictionary or commentary, and revise where the evidence points elsewhere." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/howto/5> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/howto/5> <https://schema.org/position> "5"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/howto/5> <https://schema.org/text> "Correlate the passage with the rest of Scripture, asking how other texts on the same subject, and the whole story centred on Christ, shed light on it." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/howto/6> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/howto/6> <https://schema.org/position> "6"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/howto/6> <https://schema.org/text> "Ask what the passage meant for its first readers and what it calls you to believe, pray or do now, and write down one concrete response." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/howto/7> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/howto/7> <https://schema.org/position> "7"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/howto/7> <https://schema.org/text> "Share what you found with others, ideally in a small group, so that your reading can be tested and enriched." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/inductive-bible-study/howto> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowTo> .
