<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/scripture-interprets-scripture/example> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/ontology#Rewrite> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/scripture-interprets-scripture/example> <https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/ontology#after> "Psalm 91 is a song of trust for the one who makes God their refuge; Jesus, offered this very verse as a dare, answered from Deuteronomy 6:16 that we must not put God to the test." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/scripture-interprets-scripture/example> <https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/ontology#before> "God promised his angels will catch me, so I can push my luck and he has to rescue me." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/scripture-interprets-scripture/example> <https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/ontology#situation> "Someone reads Psalm 91:11-12, \"they will bear you up in their hands\", and concludes that God guarantees protection from the consequences of any deliberate risk they choose to take." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/scripture-interprets-scripture/example> <https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/ontology#why> "In Matthew 4:5-7 the devil quotes the psalm on its own, and Jesus answers \"Again, it is written\", letting another Scripture show how the promise is meant to be held." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/scripture-interprets-scripture/howto/1> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/scripture-interprets-scripture/howto/1> <https://schema.org/position> "1"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/scripture-interprets-scripture/howto/1> <https://schema.org/text> "Read the passage in its own setting first (paragraph, chapter, book) so you know what it says before you compare it with anything else." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/scripture-interprets-scripture/howto/2> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/scripture-interprets-scripture/howto/2> <https://schema.org/position> "2"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/scripture-interprets-scripture/howto/2> <https://schema.org/text> "Look up the cross-references in a study Bible, starting with any passage the text quotes or echoes, and read each one in its own context too." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/scripture-interprets-scripture/howto/3> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/scripture-interprets-scripture/howto/3> <https://schema.org/position> "3"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/scripture-interprets-scripture/howto/3> <https://schema.org/text> "When the New Testament quotes the Old, open the Old Testament passage and ask what it meant there and why the New Testament writer reached for it." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/scripture-interprets-scripture/howto/4> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/scripture-interprets-scripture/howto/4> <https://schema.org/position> "4"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/scripture-interprets-scripture/howto/4> <https://schema.org/text> "Gather the passages that treat the same subject directly and at length, and let these clearer, fuller passages shed light on brief or puzzling ones." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/scripture-interprets-scripture/howto/5> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/scripture-interprets-scripture/howto/5> <https://schema.org/position> "5"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/scripture-interprets-scripture/howto/5> <https://schema.org/text> "Check that each link is a real link of meaning, not just a shared English word that may mean different things in different books." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/scripture-interprets-scripture/howto/6> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/scripture-interprets-scripture/howto/6> <https://schema.org/position> "6"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/scripture-interprets-scripture/howto/6> <https://schema.org/text> "When passages seem to pull in different directions, hold both and ask how they fit together before choosing one and setting the other aside." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/scripture-interprets-scripture/howto/7> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/scripture-interprets-scripture/howto/7> <https://schema.org/position> "7"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/scripture-interprets-scripture/howto/7> <https://schema.org/text> "Test your conclusion against the main lines of the whole biblical story and, as your church teaches, against its wider reading of the faith." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/bible-graph/id/scripture-interprets-scripture/howto> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowTo> .
